<section id="music">
    <h2>My Music Journey</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card card-wide">
            <h3>Music</h3>
            <div class="card-body">
                <div class="card-text">
                    <p>
                        Music has been an important part of my life for many years. I am a trombonist and musician,
                        and I continue to perform, collaborate, and explore music alongside my work in neuroscience.
                        <br><br>
                        I began playing the trombone as a 9 year-old and have since played in orchestras and bands.
                        Over the years, music has taken me through different styles, ensembles, and musical
                        experiences. Today, I continue to play and take part in musical projects whenever I can.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card card-wide">
            <h3>Music &amp; Neuroscience</h3>
            <div class="card-body">
                <div class="card-text">
                    <p>
                        My experience as a musician is also closely connected to my scientific interests. Much of my
                        research asks how the brain changes through musical learning and how musical experience is
                        represented in the brain. Being both a researcher and a musician gives me a personal
                        perspective on the questions I study.
                        <br><br>
                        My wife is a piano teacher, and watching her teach has given me a close-up view of just how
                        demanding both learning and teaching music can be — a perspective that shapes how I think
                        about my research.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

